Operating systems manage computer hardware and software resources efficiently. 
The operating system handles memory management, process scheduling, and device control.

Process scheduling ensures that CPU time is shared among processes properly. 
It improves the performance of the system.

Memory management allocates memory space and prevents conflicts between processes. 
It is a key function of operating systems.

File systems in operating systems help in storing and retrieving data. 
They organize files in a structured manner on storage devices.

Device management allows communication between the system and hardware devices. 
It controls input and output operations effectively.

Operating systems provide security features such as user authentication and authorization. 
These features protect system resources from unauthorized access.

Modern operating systems support multitasking and multi-user capabilities. 
They allow multiple applications and users to run at the same time.

Virtual memory allows the system to use disk space as additional memory. 
It improves performance when physical memory is limited.

Operating systems are designed for efficiency and reliability. 
They ensure proper utilization of available hardware resources.